{"conjecture": "An LLM can reliably generate executable code from abstract specifications if decoding is constrained by a runtime proof witness: at each generation step, the model emits not only code tokens but also symbolic proof obligations whose discharge is checked by an SMT solver, and tokens causing proof failures are masked out of the distribution. This forces the model to sample only from continuations that maintain provable consistency with the spec, yielding code that provably holds its commitments rather than merely appearing to. The approach is falsifiable: if the constraint mechanism does not significantly reduce post-hoc verification failures compared to unconstrained decoding across a benchmark of formal specifications, the conjecture is wrong."}
